-green.md", ".prawduct/changes/verify-resolutions-lineage.md", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/README.md", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/host-facts-2026-11-12.md", ".prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md", ".prawduct/runbooks/promote-a-pruned-release.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "gitignore contract: the 'must be committed' set is MANAGED_FILES (the 1.x migration REMOVE registry), so the new ignored-by-rule report names files a v2 repo never has and its 'replace' remedy clears the advisory while project-state.yaml, backlog.md and changes/ stay ignored", "recommendation": "Class, not instance. Why it broke: `_contract_diff` treats `{*MANAGED_FILES, *RETIRED_GITIGNORE_ENTRIES}` as the set prawduct needs committed, but MANAGED_FILES lists the file-sync framework files that `migrate_plugin` deletes from a consumer (`.prawduct/critic-review.md`, `pr-review.md`, `build-governance.md`, `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md`, `tools/product-hook`). Under #762's own `.prawduct/` rule, `describe_ignored_by_rule` offers `.prawduct/*` plus re-includes of `artifacts/` and three 1.x files. Once those lines are pasted, `contract-ignored-by-rule` goes quiet (`test_probe_is_inert_once_the_rule_is_narrowed` and `test_negated_reinclusion_satisfies_the_contract` pin this), but the v2 state still under `.prawduct/*` stays ignored. That includes project-state.yaml, backlog.md, the `changes/` fragments this bundle's PR gate now requires, and operator-verification.md, so the next clone never gets them. Under the very common `.claude/` rule, the advisory says prawduct 'needs committed' seven `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md` files a plugin-era repo does not have, and tells the owner to add `!.claude/skills/`, which un-ignores their personal skills (`test_probe_fires_both_when_entries_missing_and_a_rule_hides_managed_files` pins that case as intended). Fix: build the must-commit set from what a v2 product repo commits (documentation/project-structure.md: its `.prawduct/` state and `.claude/settings.json`), not from the migration registry. Then make the 'replace' remedy re-include that set, and test the effect of following the advice: paste it on a fixture and assert `git check-ignore` no longer matches project-state.yaml or a fragment.", "files": ["plugin/lib/core.py", "plugin/lib/gitignore_probes.py", "tests/test_gitignore_contract_semantics.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "test-status: a stamp from another host (ELSEWHERE) reads as 'running' forever, and every refusal and --wait timeout says 'do not start another run', which never ends", "recommendation": "Instance. `record_progress.recorder_state` returns ELSEWHERE whenever `in_progress.host != socket.gethostname()`. `record_in_progress` treats ELSEWHERE as running, `test_status(wait=True)` keeps looping on it until the timeout (exit 75: 'wait again ... do not start another run'), and `run_refusal`, which every gate prints, says 'Wait for it to record ... and do not start another'. Nothing ends that state. The recorder's real fate cannot be checked, and no age bound applies. A killed recorder whose void names another hostname therefore blocks evidence with advice that, if obeyed, never ends. On macOS, `gethostname()` follows the network-assigned name when no HostName is set, so the stamping machine and the reading machine can be the same laptop. A shared or bind-mounted checkout (container versus host) produces the same state. The learnings rule applies: 'A REMEDY named in a checklist must be tested against the predicate that produced the condition \u2014 non-terminating advice gets obeyed'. Fix: bound ELSEWHERE, for example by reading it as interrupted once elapsed time far exceeds the last `duration_seconds` or a fixed ceiling, and give its refusal text a way out (re-run when you know the other host's run is dead). Pin it with a test where the host differs and the stamp is old.", "files": ["plugin/lib/record_progress.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "PR gate: a branch that adds a valid fragment passes without the legacy-pending check, so an unreleased change-log entry (even one this branch adds beside its fragment) still merges and is then refused at the release", "recommendation": "Instance. The change record says `check-change-log-entry` now refuses what the release would refuse ('The PR gate agrees with the release'). The `legacy-pending-entry` check (`_legacy_pending_refused` on `git show HEAD:.prawduct/change-log.md`) runs only on the legacy path, after `_fragment_entry_verdict` has returned `None`. When the branch touches `.prawduct/changes/`, `_fragment_entry_verdict` answers `fragment-present` (exit 0) and the committed log is never read. So a branch that adds a fragment and also adds an unreleased `## ` entry to change-log.md, or that merges while the log already holds one, passes this gate. `check-releasability` / `cut-change-log` then refuse at the release, which is the late refusal #1125 set out to move forward. No test in tests/test_change_log_entry_gate.py covers a fragment plus a pending legacy entry. Fix: run `_legacy_pending_refused` on the committed log in the fragment path too, at least when the log is in the branch's diff, and add that fixture.", "files": ["plugin/lib/coverage.py", "tests/test_change_log_entry_gate.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "Callers of the suite verdict still write their own 'run the suite' remedy and classify the record themselves, so a running record attempt (#1178) is told to start a second run", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: #1178 put the 'a run is in progress, wait' answer in gates.record_in_progress / run_refusal and wired only transfer_remedy to it, and #724 in the same bundle added a refusal that writes its own remedy text. Every consumer that wraps tests_are_current's reason in its own 'run the suite' sentence, or reads raw record fields to classify it, gets the in-progress case wrong. Members found: (1) cmd_critic_begin's exit-7 text in plugin/bin/prawduct-hook (~L2410) prints the verdict reason, which says 'Wait for it ... and do not start another', and then 'Run the declared suite and record it'. The critic SKILL.md exit-7 row ('the suite runs, then this mode again') and review-cycle.md L139 ('run the suite, then this mode again') say the same. (2) release_readiness.check_releasability's `unproven-suite:` line (~L907) appends 'run the suite and record it' to the same reason. (3) critic_consolidate._stale_evidence_slug re-reads .test-evidence.json and labels a write-ahead void 'degraded', or 'failing' when the earlier counts were red, so the guard-refusal telemetry `stats` groups on cannot tell an in-flight run from a degraded or red one. plugin/docs/test-report-contract.md meanwhile says 'every gate's refusal says to wait for the run rather than start another', which these sites make false. Who acts wrongly: an agent at verify-resolutions or a release Phase 0 that starts a second suite on top of the first, which is the failure #1178 exists to prevent. Fix by construction, not by patching each site: have the verdict carry its remedy, or the record state (running / interrupted / stale / red / degraded) from one owner (e.g. tests_are_current returning record_in_progress's state, or a gates.suite_remedy(project_dir) every surface prints), and derive the slug from that same state. Then make the skill rows relational ('do what the printed verdict says, then this mode again'). To bound the class, grep the callers of tests_are_current, suite_vouches_for_tree and _suite_verdict for an appended 'run the suite', and every reader of `degraded`/`failed` outside gates.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/docs/test-report-contract.md"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "read-cost: once another session has written beside this one, every later Stop re-parses this session's whole transcript", "recommendation": "Instance. gates._made_by_another_session calls host.other_session_wrote_since(transcript, session_start). The `since` it passes is the session start, so once any sibling transcript has been written after that moment the answer stays True for the rest of the session. host.session_tool_activity then json-parses every line of this session's transcript. That path runs on every Stop (every turn) whenever HEAD's tree has moved since session start, which is true after the session's first commit. The cost therefore follows the transcript's size, which grows all session and reaches tens of MB in long sessions. It does not follow what the caller uses: the activity windows around the few commit epochs being classified. The cost-bound docstrings in session_work_span and _session_authored_paths describe the read as conditional but give no size bound. Bound the cost to the caller's need. Options: memoise the parsed windows per session, keyed on transcript size or mtime, and read only the appended tail on the next Stop; skip the read when every commit since session start is already attributed; or compare the sibling's mtime against the oldest commit still unclassified rather than against the session start.", "files": ["plugin/lib/host.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "blocking", "title": "Commits dropped from the session span as 'another session's' leave no trace, and a reachable false drop silently disarms the reflection and Critic Stop gates", "recommendation": "Instance (one site). Why it broke: the new exclusion in gates._session_authored_paths filters `commits`/`merges` through `_made_by_another_session` and returns only the surviving paths. Nothing records what it dropped: no field on the `session_work_span` result, no stderr note, no telemetry, and no use of the `why` list. A wrong drop is therefore indistinguishable from 'this session changed nothing'. A wrong drop is reachable. The predicate decides who RAN `git commit`, but the gates ask who AUTHORED the change. Suppose the session writes code with Edit/Write and the user commits it from a terminal between turns, while a second session in the same launch directory has written its transcript. Then those paths leave the span, and neither the reflection gate nor the Critic trigger fires for code this session wrote. The change record's sentence 'a commit made between this session's calls was not made by it' is true of the commit and not of the work. Fix: (1) make a drop observable. Carry the dropped SHAs on the span result (for example `dropped_other_session`) and surface them where the Stop observation or stderr reaches someone, so the rate of the exemption can be audited. (2) Decide whether a dropped commit whose paths this session's own Edit/Write tool calls touched still counts. The same transcript already holds those calls' `file_path` inputs. Pin whichever you choose with a test of the terminal-commit case.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/host.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "A recorder stamp naming another host is read as 'running' forever, so a dead run there makes every gate say 'wait, do not start another' with no exit", "recommendation": "Instance. `record_progress.recorder_state` returns ELSEWHERE whenever the stamp's host differs from `socket.gethostname()`. `gates.record_in_progress` and `_suite_run_in_progress` treat that as running, with no age bound, so it never becomes GONE. On macOS the hostname can change with the network (`.local` versus a DHCP-assigned name), and a container sharing the checkout also has a different one. In either case a recorder that died reads as in progress indefinitely. `test-status` prints `running:`, every gate's refusal says 'do not start another', `--wait` exits 75 and says 'wait again; do not start another run', and the PR gate's transfer note says the same. An agent that follows the text never reaches the one remedy, running the suite. Neither the change record nor docs/test-report-contract.md mentions this state: both say an interrupted run reads `stale (interrupted)` unconditionally. Fix: bound ELSEWHERE, for example by reading it as interrupted once it is well past the last recorded run's duration or a fixed cap. Or, at minimum, make the ELSEWHERE refusal name the way out ('if no run is going on <host>, run the suite') instead of 'do not start another'. Add a test for a stale foreign-host stamp.", "files": ["plugin/lib/record_progress.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "Records Pass: the #1178 consumer note promises `running:` during every record, but a worktree with no standing record gets no in-progress stamp", "recommendation": "It ships (the consumer note becomes CHANGELOG text). `.prawduct/changes/test-evidence-no-stale-green.md` says 'While `test-evidence record` is running, `test-status` prints **running**'. However, `_void_test_evidence` returns `absent` (or `unreadable`) and writes nothing when no readable `.test-evidence.json` stands, so no `in_progress` stamp exists. A fresh worktree's first record is therefore invisible: during that run `test-status` reports missing or stale evidence, `--wait` returns at once, and the /prawduct:pr skill's 'only run the suite if test-status reports stale or evidence is missing' starts the second concurrent run #1178 exists to prevent. Per-worktree records make this the case for every new worktree and clone, not an edge. Fix one of two things. Either the code: write an in-progress stamp even when no record stands, as a void-shaped stub that readers already refuse through `degraded`. Or the claim: qualify the consumer note and docs/test-report-contract.md's 'tells three states apart' to say that a first run is not announced.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/test-evidence-no-stale-green.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/test-report-contract.md"], "fid": "R-8"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 7, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation had no subject", "recommendation": "No live plan claims `integration/tech-debt-2026-11-15` (manifest scope null, `scope_unresolved_cause: unbound`), so there was no `governed_by:` disposition to check against the diff, and record_lint reports `governed-by-gap: 0`. Each fix's intent was judged against its change fragment (`files_oracle`) instead. The code largely does what the fragments say, apart from the three findings.", "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Another session's commits: a commit the user makes in this session without a tool call is disowned whenever another session wrote", "recommendation": "`host.session_tool_activity` counts only `tool_use`/`tool_result` windows, so a commit made by the user's `!` shell input or by hand in a terminal falls between calls. `_made_by_another_session` drops it once any sibling transcript was written since session start. The direction is a missed reflection or Critic fire, not a false block, and the change record states the transcript rule. No edit is wanted unless the owner wants hand commits to count.", "files": ["plugin/lib/host.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "_shared_ignore_matches counts any ignore source whose basename is `.gitignore`, including a global excludes file at `~/.gitignore`", "recommendation": "`Path(rule[0]).name == \".gitignore\"` keeps a rule from a `core.excludesFile = ~/.gitignore` setup, which is common, as if it were shared, contrary to the docstring's 'only .gitignore files count'. Comparing the source against paths inside the work tree, rather than against the basename, would close it. Low impact.", "files": ["plugin/lib/core.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Probe and fixer still read .gitignore with the locale encoding and catch only OSError", "recommendation": "`_read_gitignore` and `update_gitignore` call `read_text()` with no encoding and do not catch `UnicodeDecodeError`, so a non-UTF-8 `.gitignore` raises out of a probe that is documented as never raising. This predates the bundle, and the bundle reworked both functions without fixing it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/core.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "`native-review prepare` is listed in _EPHEMERAL_READ_ONLY_OPS although it moves HEAD and writes a sidecar", "recommendation": "The table's name is its contract, and its comment now has to say 'not read-only, admitted for the opposite reason'. The same function already handles commands admitted on a narrower promise as explicit named branches (test-evidence record, plan import-status/retract). Move prepare to a branch like those, so the read-only table stays truthful for the next reader who extends it.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The HEAD-left-branch check runs only in the explicit critic-consolidate wrapper, not in the consolidation owner", "recommendation": "head_guard is described as detection 'for any cause', but cmd_critic_consolidate is the only consolidation path that runs it. That wrapper also parses the manifest itself (_manifest_branch) instead of asking critic_consolidate. The SubagentStop and session-end-backstop consolidations skip it, so outside a native-paired review's NEXT-ACTION a moved HEAD goes unsaid. SubagentStop's stderr is not read, so the delivery channel for this is the NEXT-ACTION consolidate renders. Consider putting the manifest-branch check there for every review, not only those with a native sidecar.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/head_guard.py", "plugin/lib/native_review.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The selection of 'which review is this branch's' now depends on evidence_notes' private _branch_commits, which one verify_anchor call can run twice", "recommendation": "select_branch_review is presented as the one owner, but its input comes from evidence_notes._branch_commits, a private function now called from two sites in critic_consolidate (verify_anchor and _not_this_branchs). On a sibling-branch anchor it computes the same rev-list twice. critic_mode and critic_consolidate also now import each other lazily. Consider exporting the branch range (or the finished answer) from evidence_notes and passing it into _not_this_branchs once.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The Stop gate's work span and the next session's previous-session check now answer the same question differently", "recommendation": "cmd_stop passes transcript_path, so its span drops another session's commits. briefing._check_previous_session_gates (and hook _clear_observations) call session_work_span without it, so they still count those commits. briefing's comment 'the SAME predicate cmd_stop's Gate 1 blocks on' is now false: the next session can be warned that a reflection gate went unmet for work the Stop gate correctly exempted. The previous session's transcript is not available at clear time, so either record the Stop's span or its exemption, or make the briefing comment relational.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "operator_verification reports an unreadable state file through queue_status and makes `required` tri-state", "recommendation": "QUEUE_STATE_UNREADABLE is a fact about project-state.yaml, carried in a key named for the queue, and `required` becomes True/False/None. The one consumer (cmd_check_operator_verification) orders its checks to cope. This is localized, with two signals, so it is borderline. A separate `state_status` key, or a single verdict enum, would stop a future consumer from reading `required` before `queue_status`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/operator_verification.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "STALE_EVIDENCE_REASONS has no reader, and its `degraded` slug also covers an in-progress run", "recommendation": "The closed set is declared in critic_consolidate.py, but `_stale_evidence_slug` returns literals and no test asserts membership, so the tuple documents rather than constrains. A verify-resolutions refused while a suite run is in progress (the write-ahead void carries `degraded`) records reason `degraded` (or `failing`, if the voided counts were red). The telemetry cannot tell 'wait for the run' from a genuinely degraded record. Consider a `running` member, or pin the slug against the tuple in a test.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The transcript-format host facts the span exclusion rests on live only in a docstring and a change-record measurement", "recommendation": "host.py relies on several undocumented Claude Code facts: one folder of top-level *.jsonl transcripts per launch directory, subagent transcripts in a subfolder, tool_use/tool_result block shapes, and `run_in_background` on inputs. The change record cites '42 files, a 7.8 MB one in 70 ms' with no command that re-derives it. The repo's pattern for host facts is a dated host-facts record with a re-deriving script (native-review-paired/host-facts-2026-11-12.md and fork_costs.py). Recording these facts there would let a host upgrade be checked against them. The fail direction is mostly safe (None keeps the commit), so this is not a finding.", "files": ["plugin/lib/host.py"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Records Pass coverage", "recommendation": "Rated all 15 files_oracle records against the three bars. Covered: api-contract.md and data-model.md; the nine .prawduct/changes fragments; native-review-paired README.md and host-facts-2026-11-12.md; and both runbooks. Spot-checked their named symbols, removed refusal codes, exit codes (7, 75, 3), advisory priority, guard-fact body fields, the collateral-of validation and the gitignore basis/ignored_by_rule shape against HEAD. All held, except the test-evidence consumer note (finding above) and the ELSEWHERE omission (folded into its finding). No runbook command was found to mislead into action.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/test-evidence-no-stale-green.md", ".prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md", ".prawduct/runbooks/promote-a-pruned-release.md"], "oid": "O-12"}], "duration_seconds": 900, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `integration/tech-debt-2026-11-15`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunk": null, "base_reviewed": "6f3d232ffe46185d1f535ae5ee3270b87dee4846", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/backlog-service.md", ".prawduct/changes/gate-flags-fail-closed.md", ".prawduct/changes/gate-span-and-coverage-slips.md", ".prawduct/changes/gitignore-contract-semantics.md", ".prawduct/changes/operator-verification.md", ".prawduct/changes/release-fragments-only.md", ".prawduct/changes/review-never-moves-head.md", ".prawduct/changes/test-evidence-no-stale-green.md", ".prawduct/changes/verify-resolutions-lineage.md", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/README.md", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/host-facts-2026-11-12.md", ".prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md", ".prawduct/runbooks/promote-a-pruned-release.md", "docume